{"file":{"content":"The c=7 confirmation changes the status from “promising audit” to a concrete finite-combinatorial proof target. The live finite-side endpoint is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le 1-\\delta\n}\n]\n\nfor the threshold cascade operator. Everything else is either proved, diagnostic, or superseded as a uniformity route.\n\nMy main critique after checking the current `.tex`: the conclusion now has the right endpoint, but an earlier status list still reads as if comparison-graph expansion, short-witness coverage, and strip-classification are live peers of the cascade route. That section should be updated or explicitly marked historical/superseded. Otherwise a reader will think the paper has several unresolved finite-side conjectures rather than one sharply isolated one.\n\n## 1. What the c=7 result buys\n\nThe table\n\n[\n\\Lambda_c:\\quad 1.0392,\\ 1.0051,\\ 0.9827\n]\n\nis the first actual crossing below the Schur threshold. That matters. The previous c=6 result only suggested the line would be crossed; c=7 confirms the cascade budget is not merely asymptotically marginal in the tested range.\n\nEven better, the proof-facing diagnostics all point in the same direction:\n\n[\np_1=\\Pr(\\beta=1\\mid \\text{cascade})\\approx 0.388\n]\n\nis stable;\n\n[\n\\text{off-representative mass}: 3.6\\cdot10^{-3}\\to8.0\\cdot10^{-4}\\to1.9\\cdot10^{-4}\n]\n\nis collapsing;\n\nand the heavy row is spread, not concentrated:\n\n[\n\\text{top column }12%,\\qquad \\text{top ten }43%.\n]\n\nSo the likely proof is not a delicate signed-cancellation proof. It is a Perron/finite-state contraction proof for a positive cascade operator.\n\nThat is the best possible outcome.\n\n## 2. Do not overuse the signed/absolute ratio\n\nThe signed/absolute ratio rising to\n\n[\n0.961\n]\n\nis useful mainly as a negative diagnostic:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{signed cancellation is not the source of the gap.}\n}\n]\n\nBut it should not appear as part of the proof strategy. The proof should work entirely with the positive cascade operator\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c\\ge0.\n]\n\nThat is cleaner and stronger. The finite H23a gap then comes from\n\n[\n\\text{mass + }{1/2,1}\\text{ profile + nonpersistence},\n]\n\nnot arithmetic interference inside the cascade. This is a good simplification.\n\n## 3. Define the scaled cascade operator precisely\n\nThe scaled recurrence you found is exactly the right coordinate:\n\n[\n\\bar t_{i+1}=3\\bar t_i+\\bar x,\n]\n\nwith local weight\n\n[\n\\frac13,2^{-M_{i+1}|\\bar x|}.\n]\n\nThis should become the canonical definition of (\\mathcal K_c).\n\nA good state space is:\n\n[\nv_i=(i,\\bar t_i,\\hat r_i),\n]\n\nwhere:\n\n* (i) is the cascade level;\n* (\\bar t_i) is the scaled locked difference class, i.e.\n[\nB_i-B_i'=2\\cdot3^{c-3-i}\\bar t_i;\n]\n* (\\hat r_i) is the target/heavy-residue data needed to decide whether the transition is boundary or deeper.\n\nThen transitions are indexed by the reduced increment\n\n[\n\\bar x\n]\n\nand have weight\n\n[\nw_i(v\\to v')\n============\n\n\\frac13,2^{-M_{i+1}|\\bar x|},\\beta(v,\\bar x),\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\beta(v,\\bar x)\\in\\left{\\frac12,1\\right}.\n]\n\nThe normalized operator should be chosen so that the crude marginal model has Perron radius (1). Then the theorem is meaningfully:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta.\n]\n\nMake the normalization explicit. Otherwise a reader will not know whether (\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)<1) is structural or just a consequence of scaling choices.\n\n## 4. Build (\\mathcal K_c) before proving it\n\nThe next computational step should be matrix-level, not row-budget-only.\n\nFor (c=7,8,\\dots,30), compute:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c),\n]\n\n[\n\\sup_v \\sum_{v'}\\mathcal K_c(v,v'),\n]\n\n[\n\\text{Perron vector concentration},\n]\n\n[\n\\beta=1\\text{ mass under the Perron vector},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{off-representative mass under the Perron vector}.\n]\n\nThe row budget at (x=-1) is useful, but the Perron radius is the actual theorem target. Since the top row is (16.7\\times) the mean row, the row bound may be much worse than the spectral radius. If (\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)) is already clearly below the row sup, the proof should use a weighted Schur/Perron certificate rather than an unweighted row estimate.\n\nFor each (c), also solve for a positive vector (V_c) such that\n\n[\n\\frac{(\\mathcal K_c V_c)(v)}{V_c(v)}\n\\le 1-\\delta_c.\n]\n\nThis is the finite certificate version of Perron contraction. If the (V_c) profiles stabilize in scaled coordinates, they will tell you the symbolic Lyapunov function to prove.\n\n## 5. The three proof lemmas are exactly right\n\nThe proof should now be organized around these three lemmas.\n\n### Lemma A: (\\beta=1) channel bound\n\nYou already have:\n\n[\np_1\\le K/3<0.733\n]\n\nfrom adjacent-depth cascade mass. That proves a strict saving, but it is much weaker than the observed\n\n[\np_1\\approx0.388.\n]\n\nThe remaining constant (q) is the degenerate boundary channel. The natural target is:\n\n[\nq\\le \\frac13.\n]\n\nThis should be attacked directly. The (\\lambda=0) condition should be one nontrivial congruence modulo (3) on the leading cascade digit. If so, it costs exactly one mod-(3) condition:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\lambda=0\\mid \\text{boundary cascade})\\le\\frac13.\n]\n\nTry to prove it row-wise, not just averaged:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\lambda=0\\mid v)\\le\\frac13\n]\n\nfor every recurrent cascade state (v). If row-wise fails, prove it after one transition:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\lambda_{i+1}=0\\mid v_i)\\le\\frac13+O(3^{-i})\n]\n\nor prove a two-step version.\n\nThis lemma is the key local saving.\n\n### Lemma B: off-representative mass is (o(1))\n\nThe data suggest something much stronger than (o(1)):\n\n[\n3.6\\cdot10^{-3},\\ 8.0\\cdot10^{-4},\\ 1.9\\cdot10^{-4}.\n]\n\nThis looks roughly geometric in (c). But for the theorem, it is enough to prove:\n\n[\n|\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}|\\to0\n]\n\nor even\n\n[\n|\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}|\\le\\epsilon_0\n]\n\nbelow the contraction margin.\n\nYour structural explanation is good:\n\n[\n\\text{off-representatives can enter only in the last }O(\\log_3 c)\\text{ levels}.\n]\n\nTurn this into a bound:\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}\\le\n\\sum_{j\\le C\\log c} 3^{-j}\\cdot(\\text{tail factor})\n]\n\nor whatever the exact transition count gives. The proof target should be operator-norm/off-row mass, not just total mass.\n\n### Lemma C: heavy-state nonpersistence\n\nThis is the Perron part. The heavy row (x=-1) is the sup row, but the mass is spread:\n\n[\n\\text{top column }12%,\\qquad \\text{top ten }43%.\n]\n\nSo no single state realizes the worst case repeatedly. Prove this as a drift statement.\n\nUsing\n\n[\n\\bar t_{i+1}=3\\bar t_i+\\bar x,\n]\n\ndefine a Lyapunov function such as\n\n[\nV(\\bar t,\\hat r)=1+\\epsilon,\\ell(\\bar t,\\hat r),\n]\n\nwhere (\\ell) measures distance from the heavy aligned state. The goal is:\n\n[\n(\\mathcal K_c V)(v)\\le(1-\\delta)V(v).\n]\n\nIf the chain is most dangerous near (\\bar t=0) and (\\hat r=1), choose (V) slightly larger away from that state so that mass escaping from the heavy state receives a discount in the weighted Schur test.\n\nNumerically, use the Perron vector of (\\mathcal K_c) to guess (V). Then simplify it into a symbolic function.\n\n## 6. Prove contraction by domination, not exact computation\n\nA clean proof would dominate (\\mathcal K_c) by a limiting positive operator:\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c\\le \\mathcal K_\\infty+E_c,\n\\qquad\n|E_c|\\to0,\n]\n\nand prove\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_\\infty)<1.\n]\n\nThe scaled coordinate\n\n[\n\\bar t_{i+1}=3\\bar t_i+\\bar x\n]\n\nstrongly suggests a limiting operator on a countable state space. The local weights\n\n[\n\\frac13,2^{-M|\\bar x|}\n]\n\nare summable in (\\bar x), so (\\mathcal K_\\infty) should be compact or at least bounded on a weighted (\\ell^\\infty) space.\n\nThis may be the most elegant proof:\n\n1. define (\\mathcal K_\\infty);\n2. prove a weighted Schur bound\n[\n|\\mathcal K_\\infty|_V\\le1-\\delta;\n]\n3. show (\\mathcal K_c\\to \\mathcal K_\\infty) in the weighted norm.\n\nThis would avoid a long finite-(c) combinatorial proof.\n\n## 7. A concrete Perron-certificate workflow\n\nFor (c=10,15,20,30), compute (\\mathcal K_c) and its Perron right vector (V_c). Then inspect whether (V_c) is approximated by something simple:\n\n[\nV_c(\\bar t,\\hat r)\\approx A_{\\hat r}(1+\\lambda |\\bar t|)^{-p},\n]\n\nor\n\n[\nV_c(\\bar t,\\hat r)\\approx A_{\\hat r}\\theta^{v_3(\\bar t)}\n]\n\nor a small finite table in (\\hat r) times a simple function of (\\bar t).\n\nThen test the certificate inequality:\n\n[\n\\frac{(\\mathcal K_c V)(v)}{V(v)}\\le 1-\\delta\n]\n\nwith the guessed (V). If it works numerically for large (c), the symbolic proof becomes routine.\n\n## 8. What to do with (q)\n\nThe (q\\le1/3) estimate should be proven separately and inserted before the cascade-contraction theorem.\n\nThe likely proof:\n\n* In the boundary channel, the coset phase is determined by\n[\n\\lambda\\in\\mathbb F_3.\n]\n* The (\\beta=1) degenerate channel is\n[\n\\lambda=0.\n]\n* Express (\\lambda) as an affine nonconstant function of the leading cascade digit:\n[\n\\lambda=A(v)\\xi+B(v)\\pmod3,\n\\qquad A(v)\\ne0.\n]\n* Therefore exactly one of three values of (\\xi) gives (\\lambda=0).\n\nThis proves\n\n[\nq\\le\\frac13.\n]\n\nIf there are exceptional states with (A(v)=0), show they are off-representative or nonrecurrent. That is probably where the heavy-state nonpersistence lemma overlaps.\n\n## 9. Update the paper’s endpoint statement\n\nThe open problem can now be sharpened. Instead of only saying:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta,\n]\n\nstate the intended proof decomposition:\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c=\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm main}+\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off},\n]\n\nwith:\n\n[\n|\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}|\\to0,\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm main}\n]\n\nsatisfying:\n\n[\n\\Pr(\\beta=1\\mid v)\\le p_*<1,\n]\n\nplus heavy-state nonpersistence. This makes the open problem less black-box.\n\n## 10. Specific next moves\n\nIn order:\n\n1. **Run the (c\\le30) cascade-matrix computation.** Record (\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)), row sup, Perron vector, (\\beta=1) Perron mass, off-rep mass.\n\n2. **Prove (q\\le1/3).** This is the smallest clean lemma and likely falls by a one-mod-(3) calculation.\n\n3. **Prove off-representative (o(1)).** Use the “last (O(\\log c)) levels only” structure.\n\n4. **Extract a Perron/Lyapunov certificate.** Use numerical Perron vectors to find a simple (V).\n\n5. **Assemble the contraction theorem.**\n\nIf all five work, the finite H23a theorem closes, modulo small-(c) finite verification.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThis is now a very crisp endpoint:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{finite H23a reduces to contraction of a positive threshold cascade operator.}\n}\n]\n\nThe c=7 audit says the contraction is real and mass-profile based. The next proof is finite Perron-Frobenius bookkeeping:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tq\\le1/3,\\quad\n\t\\mathcal K_c^{\\rm off}=o(1),\\quad\n\t\\text{heavy-state nonpersistence}\n\t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\t\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)<1.\n}\n]\n\nThat is the right next push.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_23.md","numLines":456,"startLine":1,"totalLines":456},"type":"text"}